now the european commission has unveiled  its plan to  overhaul the eu's migration and asylum  system  appealing to member states to show  solidarity with one another european  countries have long disagreed about the  best way to manage  migration under the new proposals  countries like hungary and poland who  refused to accept migrants  would instead offer money equipment or  personnel to help patrol borders  the european commission president josela  fonda line says the continent must rise  to the challenge  on migration this package reflects a  fair  and reasonable balance between  responsibility  and solidarity among member states  we all share the benefits we all share  the burden this package  also reflects a very pragmatic and  realistic approach  we know that we have to build trust  among member states and  citizens confidence that we can manage  this as a union dw's alexander  phenomenon  is in brussels covering this story for  us hi alexandra  tell us more about what is in this  proposal  well as we just heard ursula fonda line  has described this proposal  as pragmatic and realistic and that is  indeed what it seems to be not an  entirely new plan revolutionary plan but  a kind of compromise acknowledging the  fact how difficult it is to find an  agreement on that issue  key takeaways here the european  commission  is abandoning the idea of mandatory  refugee quarters  requiring eu members to to take in a  certain amount of refugees or migrants  another key issue is that the european  communi  commission is saying it's important to  give up screenings for new arrivals  at the eu's external borders and that it  is  important to intensify efforts to return  those who have no right to stay  in the european union so overall i would  say  that this proposal is showing that  europe is once again  getting tougher on migrants alexander it  does really seem to be  a strategy to try to appease some of the  countries who don't want to take in  migrants so tell us more  about how some of the incentives and  compromises in this proposal work  well indeed it seems to be the strategy  here to reach out and address  the concerns of those countries who  still refuse to take in refugees and  migrants the european community  commission here is saying okay if you  are  not willing to do that that you need to  contribute in other areas key word here  is flexible  uh contributions those country will be  asked to provide money to fund  so-called reception centers or to  take over the responsibility for those  who need to be returned  to their countries because their asylum  claims were rejected  in the european union so that's an  attempt  by the european commission to to make  sure that those country  are on board and that finally an  agreement can be found  on this very sensitive issue  and alexandre just quickly what makes  the commission think that they have  the solution now because this has been  one of the most politically  sensitive issues in the european union  well when we talk to eu officials or  even german politicians they tell you  that  everywhere now everyone now understands  that  something needs to be changed and that  is their argument to say that the  chances for  an agreement right now are higher than  before  our correspondent alexander fernan  reporting for us from  brussels thank you so much  so what will this mean for migrants who  are seeking shelter  in europe let's get the perspective of  karen metz from the ngo save the  children  in brussels hi karen thank you for  joining us will this new proposal  change anything for people who are in  camps right now in greece or in italy  well um we fear that this pact uh  risk repeating some of the mistakes that  we saw in greece right now the pact  introduces a new pre-screening and  border procedure which would  um allow to member states to register  and fingerprint people and then  channel them into asylum border returns  procedures which is essentially what we  see in the hot spots in greece right now  um you know processing asylum claims or  returning people  is not so straightforward there are lots  of obstacles  and we fear that people will risk ending  up in detention  um for extended periods of time  okay so you're saying it's not much of  an improvement i mean do you think this  this new proposal will  work a lot will depend on the  willingness of  eu member states to cooperate because  even if you have these border procedures  uh  member states frontline member states  like italy greece and  um and malta will be reluctant to  cooperate if there isn't any sort of  mandatory relocation  and then they might not disembark our  ships that  have been rescued and then migrants  remain stranded  so a lot will depend on the willingness  of eu member states which in the last  five years has not been very high  indeed this has been a very divisive  issue within the european union so from  your perspective  what would be the right approach to to  create a  common approach to uh migration  well um we believe that there needs to  be a a  commonly supported solidarity mechanism  which also includes mandatory relocation  because otherwise uh member states in  the front line  will not uh cooperate we also believe  that  things can be we don't need to rely on  detention there are alternatives  um member states could immediately take  up asylum seekers at the borders  uh when they arrive without having to  place them in detention while their  asylum claims are being processed  this was actually in the dublin proposal  that was uh proposed by the european  parliament back in  2016 or 2017 but has never been picked  up  and karen you are with save the children  i have to ask i mean does there need to  be a particular  approach a strategy to help children in  camps or unaccompanied minors  yes and this is a positive thing of the  pact because  it seems that unaccompanied children and  children below the age of 12 would be  exempt from the new border procedures  that are being proposed  we of course believe that a child is  anybody below the age of 18. so we find  this this  differentiation of the age of 12 quite  arbitrary but this is good there are  some specific issues like  fast tracking family reunification maybe  providing alternatives to detention  and and improving alternative care that  are mentioned in the pact but of course  um vulnerable groups are currently also  exempt from the hot spots in greece but  they are still there so it also  it remains to be seen how this will all  work in practice  karen metz from save the children  joining us from brussels thank you very  much  you
 

